WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.260
Water's polarity gives it three jobs in the cell.

2
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.860
First, it sticks to itself through a network of hydrogen bonds, which is cohesion.

3
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.560
Second, it dissolves charged and polar molecules, wrapping each ion in a hydration

4
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.720
shell. Third, and most important for this chapter, it rejects nonpolar molecules,

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.940
and that rejection is what drives the hydrophobic effect.

6
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.500
Water is never just the solvent; it's an active player.

7
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.940
The hydrophobic effect is the exception in this chapter; it's driven mainly by

8
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.500
entropy, not by attraction.

9
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.800
The second law says total disorder never decreases.

10
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.760
When small nonpolar molecules sit alone in water, the water is pushed into more

11
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.100
ordered, cage-like arrangements around them, which is low entropy and unfavorable.

12
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.520
Cluster the nonpolar groups together and you release that caged water back into its

13
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.120
free, high-entropy state.

14
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.640
The molecules huddle because water gains disorder by pushing them together, and that

15
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.140
drives protein folding and membrane assembly.

16
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:07.903
Here's a picture for the hydrophobic effect: a crowded dance floor.

17
00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:13.383
Scattered wallflowers, isolated nonpolar molecules, force everyone else to navigate

18
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:17.023
around them, and the surrounding water settles into more ordered cages.

19
00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:22.123
Push the wallflowers into one corner and the floor opens up, and the dancers, the

20
00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:23.443
water, are free to move.

21
00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.703
The main force isn't attraction between the wallflowers.

22
00:01:27.243 --> 00:01:30.623
It's the system maximizing its freedom by getting them out of the way.

23
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:34.717
The same effect that helps fold proteins builds your membranes.

24
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:38.277
A phospholipid has two greasy tails that water won't wet.

25
00:01:38.937 --> 00:01:42.757
A single-tailed molecule is cone-shaped and packs into a sphere, a micelle.

26
00:01:43.277 --> 00:01:47.357
But a bulky two-tailed phospholipid is more cylindrical, so it can't form a tidy

27
00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:51.997
sphere; instead it lines up tail to tail into a two-layer sheet, and that sheet

28
00:01:51.997 --> 00:01:54.137
closes on itself into a sealed vesicle.

29
00:01:54.957 --> 00:01:59.077
Enzymes make lipids, proteins add them, but none holds a bilayer together.

30
00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:02.417
Water does, by refusing to touch the tails.
